%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: StayInGameText_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: cc57f5139c87a1343bea2fdf258dd1bf, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 16938021700976640
    m_Localized: "We used to joke about the apocalypse. We're not laughing anymore.
      The canteen was boringly normal at first \u2014 people eating, ducks hunting
      crumbs like little dinosaurs. Then we went into the mini-mart. Five minutes
      later, screaming. We looked through the glass and saw someone getting chased.
      Thought it was a fight. Then the red sprayed. Yeah, not a fight. The bitten
      got back up. Of course they did. The mini-mart went insane. We ran to the 5th
      floor. Made it. Campus is chaos now. Screams everywhere. Worse than the movies.
      We still have a class today, though. Guess we're skipping. The ducks? No idea.
      They probably ran faster than us."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16939959825940480
    m_Localized: "Last night was endless screaming. By dawn, everything went eerily
      quiet. Blue sky, sunny, beautiful \u2014 totally wrong for the blood stains
      on the courtyard. The ducks are still waddling around looking for worms. Zombies
      don't care about ducks, apparently. Lucky ducks. Power's still on. Social media
      is exploding \u2014 half the people are joking about no more work or school,
      the other half are crying. The army posted an announcement: hide, stay quiet,
      run if you see zombies, save food. They'll find a solution. We're supposed
      to feel better now. We don't."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16940414241030144
    m_Localized: "The canteen cats are still on the roof. The fat orange one hasn't
      moved in three days. We're not sure if it's dead or just doesn't care anymore.
      This morning, we saw someone on the other side of the courtyard. He was trying
      to reach the mango tree between the dormitory and the canteen. He didn't make
      it. A zombie grabbed his ankle before he could climb. He screamed. We watched
      from the window. We didn't help. We couldn't. By the time the zombie finished,
      three more had joined. The mangoes are still on the tree. The cats finally
      moved \u2014 they ran inside the canteen. Even they know when to be scared."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16940756185858048
    m_Localized: It's raining today. The blood on the courtyard is washing into the
      drains. Somehow that makes it look less like a crime scene and more like a
      wet campus. The cats are hiding under the canteen roof. The zombies are moving
      slower. At least we don't have to do laundry in this weather. Then we remembered
      we haven't done laundry in four days. Across from us is the other dormitory
      building. Some rooms have curtains tightly shut. Some are torn and smeared
      with blood. Some are just... empty. Hope someone is still alive over there.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16941067063476224
    m_Localized: "From our balcony, we can see the other dormitory building. Two
      floors below are university students like us. Three floors above are high school
      kids. Lucky them \u2014 they all went home to their parents before this started.
      We weren't so lucky. We stayed. For exams. For dry rations. For nothing. Now
      their windows are dark. Their rooms are empty. We hope they're safe with their
      families. We hope their families are safe. We try not to think about what \"safe\"
      even means anymore."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16941399168466944
    m_Localized: "The sky is gray today. Heavy clouds, no sun. Down below, a few
      zombies shuffle across the courtyard like they have nowhere to be \u2014 which
      is funny, because neither do we. We tried calling home. No one picked up. We
      tried again. And again. Nothing. The phone just rings into emptiness. Every
      little sound in this room now feels like a death sentence \u2014 a dropped
      spoon, a creaky chair, a cough we couldn't hold in. Funny how we used to ignore
      phone calls on purpose. Now we'd kill to hear a single \"hello.\""
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16941727293063168
    m_Localized: "Scrolling through social media today was a mistake. Bad news everywhere
      \u2014 cities falling, supply lines cut, people saying goodbye. Nothing official.
      Just panic wearing a text font. We're still holed up in our room, listening
      to the hallway like it's a living thing. Wondering when someone will come.
      If someone will come. The army said they'd find a solution. That was days ago.
      Funny thing \u2014 I used to complain about deadlines. Ten deadlines at once
      and I'd lose my mind. Now? Give me all ten. Give me finals. Give me group projects
      with the worst teammates imaginable. I'd do them all, back to back, just to
      trade this silence for a due date."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16941929722757120
    m_Localized: "From the balcony, we looked down at the garden surrounding our
      dormitory. The trees are still green. The grass is overgrown. And then we saw
      them \u2014 people, moving low and slow, sneaking toward the canteen. Not zombies.
      Survivors. Like us. They're looking for food. Canned goods, instant noodles,
      anything left behind. We used to complain about the canteen food. Too salty.
      Too expensive. Always the same dishes. Now? A single pack of stale bread would
      feel like a feast. Funny how things change. Food used to be an afterthought.
      Now it's the only thought."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16942100074414080
    m_Localized: "The sky is clear blue again \u2014 beautiful, like the world is
      mocking us. The fat orange cat is back to its old habits, waddling around the
      courtyard, trying to mess with the ducks. The ducks are not impressed. They
      hiss. The cat backs off. Then tries again. Same dance, every day. At least
      someone's having fun. We're just sitting here, scrolling through social media,
      sending messages to anyone we know. Friends. Classmates. That one guy from
      group project we never really liked. No replies. Or worse \u2014 replies from
      family members saying they haven't heard from them either. We're not sure which
      is worse: silence or confirmation."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16942351090925568
    m_Localized: "We were sitting in silence when suddenly \u2014 footsteps. Running.
      Fast. Outside in the hallway. Then a growl. Low, wet, hungry. The zombie was
      chasing someone. We didn't open the door. We didn't look through the peephole.
      We just sat there, frozen, listening. The running got louder. Then the screaming
      started. Short scream. Cut off too fast. Then nothing. Just the growl. Then
      that stopped too. We didn't sleep well last night. We didn't sleep at all."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16942564023156736
    m_Localized: "The sky is gray again. Heavy clouds hanging low like they're trying
      to touch the ground. The ducks are still here, though. Waddling around the
      courtyard like nothing has changed. We watched them for a while this morning.
      Counting them. Nine. Ten. Eleven. The same number as always. No ducks missing.
      No ducks bitten. No ducks turned into zombies. We're not sure why. Maybe zombies
      don't like duck meat. Maybe the ducks are just faster than they look. Or maybe
      \u2014 and this is the funny thought \u2014 maybe the ducks are the only ones
      who figured it out. Stay low. Stay together. Don't make noise. We're taking
      notes. Not that it's helping."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16942768130572288
    m_Localized: "We sat down today and tried to analyze everything. How many days
      has it been? Feels like weeks. Feels like years. Feels like time stopped and
      we're just\u2026 waiting. We counted our supplies again. Dry rations: low.
      Water: lower. Medicine: almost gone. Hope: undefined. We talked about escape
      plans. Down the stairs? Too many zombies. Across the rooftop? Too far. Wait
      for rescue? That's the current plan. It's not a good plan. We analyzed the
      zombies too. They're slower in the rain. Faster at night. Hungry all the time.
      We still don't know what started this. We don't know how it spreads. We don't
      know anything. But we sat down and analyzed anyway. Made us feel smart for
      about five minutes. Then reality came back."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16942897986224128
    m_Localized: "Something happened today. Not big. Not like the first day. But
      something. Around noon, we heard a loud crash from below. Glass breaking. Then
      a car alarm started screaming. It went on for minutes. Maybe hours. Time is
      blurry now. We looked out the window \u2014 a car had slammed into the canteen
      wall. The driver was gone. Or maybe never there. Zombies gathered around the
      sound, bumping into each other like confused shoppers. One of them tripped
      over a duck. The duck quacked. Loud. We held our breath. The zombies turned.
      The duck ran. The zombies followed. For a few seconds, we forgot we were scared.
      Then the alarm stopped. Silence came back. The duck made it, by the way. We
      saw it waddling away like nothing happened. Legend."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16943140479909888
    m_Localized: "Last night we heard scratching at the door. Not loud. Not urgent.
      Just... slow. Deliberate. Like something knew we were in here. We didn't move.
      Didn't breathe. The scratching stopped. Then started again. Closer to the floor
      this time. We looked at each other in the dark. No one spoke. After an hour,
      it stopped. This morning, we checked the door from the window \u2014 couldn't
      see anything. But the smell. There's a smell now. Sweet. Rotten. Wrong. It's
      coming from the hallway. We don't open the door anymore. Not for anything."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16943457598652416
    m_Localized: 'We woke up this morning and someone said: "Yesterday was Valentine''s
      Day." We all went quiet. None of us remembered. Not a single person. A year
      ago, we would''ve been annoyed by couples everywhere, posts on social media,
      chocolate on sale. Now? Zombies don''t care about Valentine''s. Neither do
      we. The only heart we''re worried about is still beating in our chests. The
      ducks probably had a better Valentine''s than us. At least they still have
      each other. We have dry rations and a locked door. Happy late Valentine''s.
      Hope yours was better. Ours was just another day of trying not to die.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16943820917653504
    m_Localized: "The air feels different today. Thicker. Heavier. We don't know
      if it's in our heads or real. Some people on social media are saying the zombies
      are toxic now. That the air near them is bad. That you shouldn't get close.
      Not just because they'll bite you \u2014 but because breathing near them might
      be enough. We don't know if it's true. But we closed the window anyway. Taped
      the gaps with whatever we could find. Duct tape. Old clothes. A poster from
      the wall. The room smells like sweat and fear now. Better than the alternative.
      Outside, the ducks are still fine. Of course they are."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16944155614724096
    m_Localized: A small storm rolled in last night. Rain hammering the roof, wind
      howling through the cracks in the windows. We haven't seen the cats in a while.
      Not the fat orange one. Not the others. They're not on the canteen roof. Not
      under the trees. Not anywhere we can see. Maybe they found shelter. Maybe they
      didn't. The storm passed by morning, leaving everything wet and grey. The ducks
      are back, though. Shaking off water like nothing happened. We keep looking
      for the cats. Still nothing. We pretend it doesn't worry us. It does.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16944381268279296
    m_Localized: "We started talking about food today. Not the dry rations we've
      been eating for almost three weeks. Real food. Someone mentioned pho. The smell
      of broth in the morning, lime on the side, satay floating on top. Another person
      said banh mi \u2014 crispy bread, pate, that little thrill when you bite into
      a hidden piece of pork. Someone else just whispered \"fried rice.\" We all
      went quiet. We used to complain about the canteen food. Too oily. Too plain.
      Same thing every day. Now? We'd kill for a plate of that terrible fried rice.
      We'd kill for anything that isn't dry and wrapped in plastic. The ducks outside
      are eating worms. Even they eat better than us now."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16944646780305408
    m_Localized: Despair is creeping in. We don't talk about it, but we all feel
      it. Last night, someone started humming a song. We didn't know the lyrics.
      Just the melody. Something from a singer we used to listen to before all this.
      We couldn't even remember their name. Just the voice. Just the feeling. That's
      how far gone we are. Across from us, the other dormitory building is still
      silent. Curtains shut. No movement. No lights. It's too quiet. The kind of
      quiet that comes right before something breaks. We keep staring at those dark
      windows, waiting for a sign. Any sign. Nothing yet. Maybe that's the sign.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16947266936848384
    m_Localized: "We finally had to do it. Open the door. Not the hallway \u2014
      just the common area between our room and the other. Our room is on the left.
      Each side has its own bathroom. Not shared. But our water is running low, so
      we wanted to check. Maybe the other side still has pressure. Maybe we can store
      some for later. We opened the door slow. Creaked it. Listened. Nothing. Walked
      to the other bathroom. Turned the faucet. A sad cough. A sputter. Then nothing.
      Water's gone there too. We stood there for a minute, staring at the dry pipe
      like it owed us an apology. At least the electricity is still on. Small mercies.
      Then we heard it. Whispering. From the room next door \u2014 the other side
      of the common area. Soft. Fast. Like two people arguing without words. We knocked.
      Quietly. No answer. The whispering stopped. We knocked again. Nothing. Just
      silence. We went back to our room and locked the door. Probably imagination.
      Probably. But we didn't sleep well anyway."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16948312274845696
    m_Localized: "We sat in silence today. No reason. Just felt heavy. Someone said,
      \"I wish I learned how to survive.\" Not running from zombies \u2014 that part
      we figured out. But real survival. Finding water. Making fire. Knowing which
      plants are safe to eat. We used to scroll through survival videos on social
      media. Watched people build shelters in the woods with nothing but a knife
      and confidence. Thought it was cool. Never thought we'd need it. Now we're
      here. Day 21. No water in the pipes. No food except dry rations. No skills
      except hiding and waiting. Funny how the internet makes you feel prepared for
      everything. Then reality shows up and you realize you don't know anything at
      all."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16949406677811200
    m_Localized: "We gathered all our courage today. Walked across the balcony to
      the next room. Our room and that room share the same balcony \u2014 left and
      right. No climbing. Just a few steps. The window was dark. Curtains pulled
      tight. Something was pressed against them \u2014 and against the door. Blocking
      it. No way in. But we heard someone in there. A soft shuffle. A breath. Maybe.
      We called out. Whispered, really. No answer. We went back to our room. Told
      ourselves: if someone was alive in there, we would have met by now. Twenty-two
      days. Same floor. Same balcony. Thin walls. They would have knocked. We would
      have heard. But then again... maybe they're scared. Maybe we are too. Is finding
      another person a good thing anymore? Or just another thing to lose?"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16950869101592576
    m_Localized: The sky is clear blue today. Beautiful. Cruel. We're lying on the
      floor, staring up through the window. On a day like this, back when things
      were normal, we'd be inside playing games. A few rounds of something. Then
      cook spicy noodles. Eat until our stomachs burned. Laugh about nothing. Now
      we just lie here. Same sky. Same sun. Same us. But different. Everything is
      different. The birds are still flying. The clouds are still moving. The world
      didn't stop. We did. We look up and forget, for a second, that there's blood
      on the courtyard. Then we remember. And we keep staring at the sky anyway.
      There's nothing else to do.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16951945443885056
    m_Localized: "We went on social media today. Just to kill time. To feel less
      alone. There's a new trend going around \u2014 people filming themselves running
      from zombies. Like a challenge. \"Adapt or die,\" they say. Run fast. Film
      it. Post it. Some of them are fast. Some are lucky. But we started noticing
      something. The people who posted videos last week? They're offline now. All
      of them. We went back and watched again. Paused. Zoomed in. A scratch here.
      A bite mark there. Hidden in the blur. Maybe they didn't notice. Maybe they
      didn't care anymore. The trend keeps going. New faces every day. Same scratches.
      Same silence after. We closed the app. Didn't feel less alone anymore."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16964717477052416
    m_Localized: 'The army posted two announcements today. The first one sounded
      like good news: they''ve established a safe zone. Anyone still alive should
      try to get there. That''s where the good news ends. The safe zone is 100 kilometers
      away. We don''t have a car. We don''t have anything that moves. Walking means
      crossing streets full of zombies. Might as well be on the moon. The second
      announcement was worse. They said the power might start going out. In five
      days. Maybe less. We stared at the screen for a long time. No one spoke. A
      hundred kilometers away. Five days of electricity left. We''re not going anywhere.
      And soon, we won''t even have light.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16968342676914176
    m_Localized: "Ever since yesterday's announcement, we've been using electricity
      like we never appreciated it before. Kettle on. Laptop charging. Phone plugged
      in. Every socket full. If we can't boil water, we can still eat dry rations
      \u2014 they're dry for a reason. But warm water? That would be nice. Just to
      feel something hot going down. A little comfort. We used to take hot water
      for granted. Tea. Instant coffee. Even just a warm cup to hold in your hands.
      Now we're thinking about the phones too. When the power goes, the phones die.
      When the phones die, no more social media. No more news. No more hoping for
      a message from someone. No more anything. We're not ready for that silence."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16969522211667968
    m_Localized: 'Someone once said, "Man is least himself when he talks in his own
      person. Give him a mask, and he will tell you the truth." Definitely not Einstein.
      Probably some writer we forgot the name of. But we''ve been thinking about
      it. Right now, these four walls are our mask. And the truth is: we''re scared.
      The second disaster is coming. Five days until the power goes out. Now four.
      We''re counting down. Every morning we wake up and think, one less day. The
      walls feel closer than yesterday. The silence feels heavier. We used to want
      to go outside. Now we''re terrified of what''s out there. But we''re also terrified
      of staying in here. There''s no good choice. Just less bad ones.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16971826625200128
    m_Localized: "We welcomed the new day with rain. Even in an apocalypse, the weather
      here never changes \u2014 scorching sun one day, pouring rain the next. Still
      humid. Still suffocating. At least the rain cools things down a bit. From our
      window, we saw zombies on the other dormitory's hallway. Shuffling. Slow. Their
      heads twitching more than usual. Rain makes them restless, maybe. Or more alert.
      We know because we saw some people down in the garden \u2014 survivors, carrying
      weapons, sneaking between the trees. They were looking for something. Food,
      probably. We thought about calling out to them. But who knows what they want?
      If they find us, maybe they take our supplies. Maybe they're friendly. Maybe
      they're not. So we just watched. Said nothing. Stayed quiet. The rain covered
      their footsteps. We stayed still. Not a sound."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16973811097223168
    m_Localized: "Thanks to the rain yesterday, we managed to collect quite a bit
      of rainwater. It will probably help us stretch our supplies for a few more
      days, but the despair of losing power doesn\u2019t feel any less heavy. We're
      trying to write down essential information from our phones onto paper, just
      in case. The power banks are fully charged. We keep trying to comfort ourselves.
      After all, what could be worse than this?"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16981242560856064
    m_Localized: "We were enjoying the cool air from the fan when \u2014 ph\u1EE5t.
      Everything stopped. The fan blades slowed. The hum died. Silence. Not complete
      silence, though. Now we could hear the leaves rustling outside. The wind blowing
      through the hallway. The birds \u2014 loud, sharp, annoying. We never noticed
      them before. Now they're all we hear. We knew this day would come. The announcement
      said five days. It's been exactly five days. Still, standing there in the sudden
      stillness\u2026 it hit harder than expected. No more lights. No more charging.
      No more fan. Just us, the heat, and whatever comes next. From now on, we survive
      for real. No backups. No comfort. Just raw, tired, hungry survival. The real
      test starts now."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16982939374927872
    m_Localized: "We used to keep a small nightlight on. Just to know we still had
      power. A tiny glow in the corner. Proof that we weren't completely cut off.
      Now it's gone. No light. No proof. This morning, the ducks started quacking.
      We've been ignoring them for days, but today we listened. I guess they're our
      favorite show now. Still the same \u2014 waddling around the garden like they
      own the place. Carefree. Arrogant. On our campus, these ducks fear nothing.
      Sometimes they chase students for food. We call them the \"campus gangsters.\"
      The boss. The don. But something felt off. We counted. One missing. Not the
      fat one. Someone else. We don't want to think about it. We've been hungry for
      too long. If anyone in the other dorms caught that duck for grilled meat\u2026
      well. That would be very disrespectful. Hilarious. But disrespectful."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16986773501460480
    m_Localized: "Last night, the moon was full and bright. With the power gone,
      it's become our only hope at night \u2014 just enough light to see each other's
      faces. The zombies can't see well in the dark either, apparently. So we left
      the curtains cracked open. Just a little. Let the moonlight slip in. It wasn't
      much. But it was something. We still keep our routine \u2014 calling home every
      three days. A thin hope that someone will pick up. Mom. Dad. Anyone. But all
      we get is ringing. Endless ringing. No voicemail. No answer. Just the hum of
      a line going nowhere. We hang up and tell ourselves they're fine. They have
      to be."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17029210672414720
    m_Localized: "We started thinking today. Our university is in the middle of nowhere.
      Isolated. Far from everything. By now, anyone still alive would have either
      holed up somewhere safer or left completely. Who would come all the way out
      here looking for food? No one, probably. And yet\u2026 we keep hoping. Watching.
      Listening for any sign of another existence. A voice. A footstep. A light in
      another window. Just proof that we're not the only ones left. The sky is cloudy
      today. Heavy. Full. Like our heads. Too many thoughts. Too many questions.
      Not enough answers. We sat in silence for a long time. Just staring out the
      window. Waiting for something that might never come."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17030520339644416
    m_Localized: The heat is unbearable. No fan. No air conditioning. Nothing to
      push the hot air around. We lay on the floor all day, not moving. Moving makes
      you sweat. Sweating makes you thirsty. Thirsty means drinking water. We don't
      have enough water. So we lie still. Arms spread. Eyes open. Staring at the
      ceiling. Then the sky through the window. Same blue. Same sun. Same heat. The
      clouds don't even bother passing by anymore. We don't talk. Talking wastes
      energy. Energy wastes water. We just exist. Breathing. Waiting. Watching the
      light shift across the floor. Hour by hour. That's our whole day now. Surviving
      by doing nothing.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17031071206948864
    m_Localized: "We heard something today. From far away. A crash. Then another.
      Metal? Wood? Hard to tell. Could be survivors. Could be zombies. Could be nothing.
      We listened for a long time. No more sounds came. To distract ourselves, someone
      picked up an old book we never finished. It's about a boy stranded at sea with
      animals. He drifts. He waits. Toward the end, he starts seeing things. Hallucinations.
      Talking to himself. Having conversations with no one. We read a few pages and
      put it down. We hope we're not going crazy. Not yet, at least. But days like
      this \u2014 heat, silence, waiting \u2014 make you wonder how long a mind can
      last."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17031777032814592
    m_Localized: Daytime used to mean safety. We could see the zombies coming. We
      could avoid any incoming dangers. Now the sun is just another enemy. Not harsh
      today. Just... annoying. The kind of heat that doesn't burn but makes you restless.
      Irritable. Stuck in your own skin. We lay around, not moving, just sweating
      in silence. Someone whispered, "I'd kill for a vegetable smoothie right now."
      Cold. Green. Fresh. We all laughed. Then stopped. Because we realized we weren't
      really laughing. Just exhaling harder than usual. The sun crawled across the
      floor. Slow. Deliberate. Mocking us. We closed the curtains halfway. Less light,
      less heat. Less hope too.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17032680225849344
    m_Localized: 'Someone asked today: "Are we going to make it?" Silence. That was
      the answer. No one said yes. No one said no. We just sat there, staring at
      nothing. The sky is overcast again, but the heat doesn''t care. Still thick.
      Still heavy. Still choking. We miss the fan like an old friend. A best friend.
      The one who always had your back. Now we just lie here, slowly getting cooked.
      Human sausages in our own sweat. We''re scared of the sun now. Hiding in the
      shadows. Pulling the curtains shut. Crawling away from any stray beam of light.
      We''re basically vampires at this point. Sad, hungry, sweaty vampires. Without
      the cool capes.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17033540402102272
    m_Localized: "We're down to one meal a day now. No one has energy for small talk
      anymore. Just eating. Just surviving. Then \u2014 somehow \u2014 the smell
      hit us. Roasted pork. Floating in the air. Sweet, savory, the kind of smell
      that used to pull us out of bed. Then the dipping sauce. Fish sauce, lime,
      chili, sugar \u2014 the perfect balance. Then garlic stir-fried morning glory.
      Just the scent of garlic hitting hot oil. Our stomachs started a riot. Protesting
      against the dry rations. Cursing them. We started daydreaming \u2014 if only
      someone could compress all those amazing dishes into dry ration form. We would
      invest immediately. Even without an apocalypse. We laughed at the thought.
      Then went back to chewing our dry blocks in silence. The smell was gone by
      noon. Probably just our imagination. Probably."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17034553146163200
    m_Localized: We saw smoke today. Far in the distance. A thin gray column rising
      against the dull sky. Could be people. Could be a campfire. Could be a house
      burning. Could be nothing. We stared at it for hours. Watched it fade. Watched
      it disappear. Didn't know whether to feel hope or fear. Maybe both. Maybe neither.
      We're tired of guessing. We sit here wishing for rain. A real storm. The kind
      that soaks everything. Washes the dust off our skin. Drowns out the thoughts.
      The worries. The dread. Just for a few hours. Let the thunder scream so we
      don't have to. Let the water hide our tears. We don't even know if we have
      any tears left. We hope tonight. Or tomorrow. Rain would be nice. Rain would
      feel like the world still cares. Even if it doesn't.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17035949656457216
    m_Localized: "The rain came. Not just a drizzle \u2014 a real storm. Thunder,
      lightning, wind tearing through the trees. Last night there were no stars.
      We knew this morning it would rain. And it did. We ran to the balcony \u2014
      well, walked quickly \u2014 and let the rain pour over us. Buckets of it. Cold,
      hard, wonderful. Our bodies felt like they were being washed clean. The dust.
      The sweat. The days of stillness. Gone. Just for a moment. Our heads felt lighter
      too. The thoughts didn't disappear, but they quieted down. We don't know how
      many more days we have to survive. 100? Less? More? We still don't know. But
      right now, standing in the rain, feeling alive for the first time in weeks\u2026
      this is enough. This is more than enough."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17037122425798656
    m_Localized: "The rain stopped. The air is fresh again \u2014 cleaner than it's
      been in weeks. We stood on the balcony, breathing it in, feeling almost human.
      We let ourselves enjoy it. Maybe too much. This morning, we looked down at
      the garden. The mud was soft. And there they were \u2014 footprints. Clear
      as day. Not ours. The rain was heavy last night. It should have washed everything
      away. But these prints were fresh. Which means someone walked through here
      after the rain stopped. They circled around the garden. Looking. Searching.
      Not just one person. A group. We've been so lost in our own relief, we forgot
      to watch. We won't make that mistake again."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17038672481173504
    m_Localized: "Last night, something woke us up. Not a scream. Not a growl. Just
      a thud. Then another. Like a stick hitting something. Slow. Deliberate. We
      had pulled the curtains shut after seeing the footprints yesterday \u2014 didn't
      want anyone looking in. But the sound traveled through the walls anyway. Clear.
      Close. Too close. We sat up in the dark, listening. Holding our breath. The
      thuds stopped. Then started again. Closer. We didn't sleep after that. Just
      sat there, backs against the wall, eyes on the door. The fear came back \u2014
      the kind we thought we had buried weeks ago. It ate through our exhaustion.
      We stayed awake all night. Waiting. Watching. Nothing happened. But nothing
      has ever felt so loud."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17039376998416384
    m_Localized: "We gathered our courage and cracked the door open. Just a sliver.
      Enough to see the hallway. Empty. Silent. But not a normal silence \u2014 the
      kind that makes your skin crawl. Like something was holding its breath too.
      We couldn't shake the feeling that they knew we were inside. Watching. Waiting.
      Maybe counting how much food we have. How many of us. How weak we look. We
      pulled the door shut. Locked it. Sat down in the corner. No one spoke. No one
      moved. Even our breathing went quiet \u2014 shallow, careful, like the door
      might hear us. The walls are thin. The silence is thicker. We stayed like that
      for hours. Just listening. Just waiting. Just hoping the door wouldn't make
      a sound."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17042726284378112
    m_Localized: "We thought about moving the wardrobe against the door. Block it.
      Keep them out. But if we block the door, we block our only exit. And it would
      tell anyone outside \u2014 yes, someone's in here. Worth hiding from. Worth
      coming back for. We're on the 5th floor. There's no other way out. Just this
      door. Just this room. Less than fifty square meters. Our only safe place. Our
      only trap. The sky is overcast today. Gray. Heavy. The kind of clouds that
      promise a thunderstorm tonight. We sat by the window, watching the light fade.
      Thinking about the door. The wardrobe. The rain that's coming. We still haven't
      moved the furniture. But we haven't ruled it out either."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17043873380384768
    m_Localized: "Last night, the rain came down hard. Thunder, wind, water pounding
      the roof. But underneath all that noise \u2014 we heard footsteps. Coming up
      the stairs. Slow. Careful. Then whispers. Not words. Just sounds. Voices trying
      to be quiet. Our hearts stopped. Then raced. Then stopped again. Every nerve
      in our bodies pulled tight. We stood by the door. Listening. The footsteps
      stopped somewhere down the hall. The whispers faded. But we didn't move. We
      sat against the door all night. Backs to the wood. Eyes on the dark. Waiting
      for something to knock. Nothing did. But we didn't sleep. Not for a second."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17044983067394048
    m_Localized: "We cracked the curtain on the balcony and looked down. A group
      of men were walking through the garden. Heavy backpacks. Arms full of things
      \u2014 blankets, pillows, bags of what looked like food. From the canteen,
      maybe. They must have raided it. Found whatever was left. We watched them move
      from shadow to shadow. Careful. Fast. They knew what they were doing. We don't
      have that kind of courage. Not anymore. Maybe not ever. So we sat back down.
      Picked up our dry rations. Chewed in silence. No blankets. No pillows. Just
      the same hard blocks and the taste of regret. We could have gone out there.
      Could have tried. But we didn't. And now we just watch."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17048466940182528
    m_Localized: The idea of leaving this room to join that group began to spark
      in our minds. Following them might mean a higher chance of survival, a way
      out of this constant, suffocating fear. But what if we have to obey a ruthless
      leader? What if they force us to do terrible, cruel things? The thought only
      twists our minds into a deeper knot, leaving us utterly lost on what to do
      next.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17049198263222272
    m_Localized: "A scream ripped through the air. Sudden. Sharp. It made us jump.
      Then came the growls \u2014 zombies. They had heard the noise too. More screams.
      Not just one voice. Several. Then banging. Cursing. The sound of something
      heavy hitting flesh. A man shouted. Another cried out. Then \u2014 the worst
      sound. A wet, tearing noise. Then crying. Not fighting anymore. Just crying.
      We pressed ourselves against the wall, hands over our mouths. Not a sound from
      us. Not a breath. The noises faded after a while. The growls stopped. The crying
      stopped. Everything stopped. Today was hell. Actual hell. Right outside our
      door. We didn't see it. But we heard every second. And we'll never unhear it."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17050447100461056
    m_Localized: "A scream ripped through the air. Sudden. Sharp. It made us jump.
      Then came the growls \u2014 zombies. They had heard the noise too. More screams.
      Not just one voice. Several. Then banging. Cursing. The sound of something
      heavy hitting flesh. A man shouted. Another cried out. Then \u2014 the worst
      sound. A wet, tearing noise. Then crying. Not fighting anymore. Just crying.
      We pressed ourselves against the wall, hands over our mouths. Not a sound from
      us. Not a breath. The noises faded after a while. The growls stopped. The crying
      stopped. Everything stopped. Today was hell. Actual hell. Down in the garden.
      Right below our window. We didn't see it. But we heard every second. And we'll
      never unhear it."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17051610134822912
    m_Localized: "We couldn't sleep last night. Every time we closed our eyes, we
      heard the screams again. The wet sounds. The crying. When dawn finally came,
      we pulled the curtain open. The garden was a massacre. The green leaves were
      painted red \u2014 dark now, dried and stained. Bodies. Not zombies. Human.
      Torn apart. We stared for a long time. No one spoke. If we had seen it with
      our own eyes instead of just hearing it\u2026 we would never leave this room
      again. Not that we want to. We sat in silence the whole day. Thinking. About
      life. About people. About why we're still here. What we're surviving for. We
      didn't find any answers. But we couldn't stop thinking anyway."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17052569925795840
    m_Localized: "We keep the curtains shut now. Tight. No cracks. No light. We don't
      want to see outside anymore. The garden. The red. The silence that followed.
      In the darkness, the thoughts come. They don't knock. They just pour in. Memories.
      Regrets. Questions that have no answers. Then the tears came. Quiet at first.
      Then harder. We couldn't stop them. We didn't want to. And then \u2014 the
      dangerous thoughts. Why keep going? Why keep fighting? Maybe it would be easier
      to just\u2026 stop. Let go. We thought about it. Longer than we should have.
      But we're still here. Still breathing. Still staring at the dark. We don't
      know if that makes us strong or just too scared to leave."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17054413288849408
    m_Localized: We pulled the curtain open again. Just a little. The red stains
      on the garden walls are darker now. Dried. Settled into the cracks. They look
      almost black in this light. We took out the old book again. The boy stranded
      on a boat with a tiger. Either go into the water and become shark food, or
      stay on the raft and wait for the tiger to eat him when hunger wins. Between
      them is just a few feet of wooden planks. That's it. No walls. No door. Just
      trust and fear and nowhere else to go. Sound familiar? We're on the 5th floor.
      Below us is death. Around us is silence. Behind that door is maybe the same.
      We don't have a tiger. But we have each other. And sometimes that's enough.
      Sometimes it's not.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17055232415449088
    m_Localized: "To kill time, we started writing names on scraps of paper. Friends.
      Classmates. People we used to borrow notes from. The girl who always shared
      her lunch. The guy who fell asleep in every lecture. We wrote down little things
      \u2014 their laugh, their bad jokes, the way they walked. Small memories. Nothing
      important. But writing them down felt like keeping them alive. We probably
      won't survive this. We don't expect anyone to read these pages. That's not
      why we're writing. It's just\u2026 pulling out memories helps us forget. For
      a moment, we're not in this room. Not hungry. Not scared. Just remembering.
      And that's enough for today."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17056515440459776
    m_Localized: "It was the end of January. The first day back after holiday break.
      We were so excited that we came early \u2014 wanted to see our friends again.
      Catch up. Laugh about nothing. That morning, everything was normal. Then it
      wasn't. The lecture hall didn't welcome us. The professors didn't either. What
      greeted us was something we'll never forget. Some of our friends decided to
      skip the first few days. Stay home a little longer. Lucky them. Were they right?
      We don't know. Maybe they're safe somewhere. Maybe they're not. Maybe coming
      back early was the worst decision we ever made. But we didn't know. No one
      knew. And now it's too late to go back."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17057436421537792
    m_Localized: 'Check today:

      - Food: OK

      - Supplies: OK

      - Room:
      OK

      - Weather: Clear sky

      - News: None

      Nothing else. Not
      hungry. Not full. Just... fine. Fine is fine. Tomorrow will be the same. Why
      write anymore? I don''t know. But I''m still writing. That''s something. I
      guess.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17058595156738048
    m_Localized: 'Things to do after this:

      - Go home. One time. At least.

      -
      Eat everything. Every dish. All of them.

      - Travel somewhere far. Anywhere.
      Just not here.

      - Learn survival stuff. Real survival. Not watching videos.

      -
      Live without worrying. What''s the point? Tomorrow might not come anyway.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17059989490188288
    m_Localized: 'Check today:

      - Rain. Blood stains washed away.

      -
      Ducks passed through the garden. Only 3 left this time.

      - Air: Cool.

      -
      5th floor isn''t that high. Maybe.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17061652716273664
    m_Localized: 'Check today:

      - Sky overcast. Wind blowing hard. Dark clouds
      coming back.

      - Ducks running. Scattered.

      - Something outside
      broke. Knocked over by the wind.

      - Feels like a good time to stop.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17062383200452608
    m_Localized: 'Check today:

      - Noise somewhere on the 5th floor. Not ours.
      Not sure where.

      - Wind is strong. Green outside. Almost too green.

      -
      The boy and the tiger made it to an island. Guess he survived. We won''t.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17063163479408640
    m_Localized: "THE POWER IS BACK!!! WHAT THE HELL IS HAPPENING?! We couldn't believe
      our eyes. That old fan in the corner suddenly made a noise \u2014 then the
      blades started spinning. Are we hallucinating? No. This is real. We plugged
      in our phones. The laptop. Everything. Army announcement: they've launched
      their forces. With everyone's help, they're moving from place to place. Clearing
      zombies. Rescuing survivors. They're about 300 kilometers away right now \u2014
      went north first. That's fine. That's more than fine. Just knowing there's
      a chance. We're not giving up. Not anymore. Not when hope is finally here."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17064395803672576
    m_Localized: "A whole night with the fan spinning lazily above us. Pure bliss.
      The nightlight is back too. We said goodbye to the moonlight that kept us company
      all those dark nights. Not forever \u2014 we'll still see it every evening.
      Just... nice to have a choice now. This morning, we tried calling home again.
      Still busy. Still no answer. But maybe they've reached somewhere safe. Maybe
      they just forgot their phone. They'll call back. They'll call back. They have
      to. The sky is clear blue today. A good sign. We're choosing to believe that."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17066298042171392
    m_Localized: "Surprisingly, the servers are still running. Well, why wouldn't
      they? Zombies don't eat internet cables. Only sharks do =)) Scrolling through,
      we saw people posting \u2014 asking if anyone else is still alive. Some places
      are holding up. Others have gone completely silent. No replies. No signals.
      Just... nothing. But for us, everyone's in high spirits. Strange, isn't it?
      We've lost so much. We're hungry. We're tired. We're not even sure what's left.
      But just being alive \u2014 after all of this \u2014 feels like a miracle already.
      So we're smiling today. Not because things are good. But because we're still
      here to smile at all."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17068905175400448
    m_Localized: "The army announced they've sent more people to different areas.
      We hope they reach us soon. As soon as possible. The ducks are back \u2014
      swarming the garden, hunting for food like nothing ever happened. And someone
      we haven't seen in a long time \u2014 the cats. They're thinner now. Noticeably
      thinner. Looks like they've burned through quite a bit of that fat reserve
      they used to flaunt. Still, they're alive. That's more than we can say for
      many. The zombies are still wandering somewhere on campus. We can hear them
      sometimes. But it's okay. We've waited two months. Two whole months. A few
      more days won't kill us. We hope."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17070198082191360
    m_Localized: "We woke up to noise. Voices. Human voices. Could the army be here
      already? No. Not them. A group of scruffy-looking people. Weapons in hand.
      They fought their way into the campus \u2014 taking down zombies like it was
      nothing. But they didn't look like the good kind. One of them was chasing the
      ducks. For fun. Or for food. Either way, we didn't want to find out. We pulled
      the curtains shut. Quiet. Don't move. Don't breathe. Please don't come up here.
      Please."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17070372003201024
    m_Localized: "We overheard them from the balcony. That group \u2014 they're looters.
      A gang. Taking advantage of the chaos. Doing whatever they want. But if the
      army catches them now, they're done for. Some people really stoop that low.
      We covered our mouths. Pressed our backs against the wall. Not a sound. One
      noise \u2014 just one \u2014 and they'd know we're here. We can't fight them.
      We barely have the strength to stand. So we stay quiet. We'll tell the army
      later. When they come. If they come. Just... not now. Not yet."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17071525948506112
    m_Localized: That was a horrible night.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 17072048684613632
    m_Localized: "They came at dawn. Not the looters \u2014 the army. Real soldiers.
      Trucks, uniforms, guns that looked like they meant business. We almost cried
      just seeing them. We told them everything. About the group. The weapons. They
      nodded. Took notes. Said they'd handle it. Then they loaded us onto a truck.
      Blankets. Water. Someone handed us a warm meal \u2014 first hot food in weeks.
      We didn't even say thank you. Just ate. Cried a little. Watched our campus
      get smaller in the distance. The ducks were still there. The cats too. We found
      out later \u2014 survivors from our university? Only a handful. Count on one
      hand. The ones who joined other groups, thinking they'd be safer together?
      Most of them didn't make it. We were the lucky ones. We don't feel lucky. But
      we're alive. After 67 days. We're alive."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 30515385299058688
    m_Localized: "We crept out to the balcony today. And guess what \u2014 there's
      a bit of green growing in the corner of the wall. Normally, we'd be too lazy
      to clean it, and it just looked... green. But today, it looks strangely edible.
      Hmm. Should we get some vegetable vitamins?"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 30516114797576192
    m_Localized: We found a little green plant growing in the corner of the balcony
      wall. Normally we'd ignore it. But now... it looks kinda tasty. Should we eat
      it?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 30516805293260800
    m_Localized: We used to ignore the little plant in the corner. Now we're dating
      it. Should we eat it?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 30518344602828800
    m_Localized: Tastes amazing. Or maybe we're just so hungry that anything tastes
      good? Either way, our energy is back up. At least we can hold on... for a while,
      right?! Hope we don't get sick haha.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 30874314264756224
    m_Localized: 'Lesson learned: don''t eat weird things unless you''re ready to
      meet your maker =))'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 30878239592235008
    m_Localized: "No matter how hungry we get, we must stay righteous. No eating
      random stuff =))\r"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
